/- ========================================================================= -/
/- Simple formulation of group theory with a type of "(A)group".             -/
/- ========================================================================= -/
/- File: HolLibrary/GroupTheory.lean

  Lean4 translation by Chris Walton 2026

  A translation of HOL Light's `Library/grouptheory.ml`, by hand, in file
  order, in the style of HolLibrary/RingTheory.lean (see there and
  HolMath/Base.lean for the conventions).  grouptheory.ml begins with
  `needs "Library/frag.ml"` (used eventually for free Abelian groups),
  `needs "Library/card.ml"` (cardinal arithmetic in a few places) and
  `needs "Library/prime.ml"` (elementary number-theoretic lemmas); the
  theorems it uses from them and from the core library are supplied via the
  CoreThms structures (SessionLibrary.GroupTheory fills them), and frag.ml's
  FRAG_MODULE via GroupTheoryDeps.  Each section, or part of a long section,
  is a bundle (a `structure`) built by a `setup` function;
  SessionLibrary/GroupTheory.lean builds the bundles in order and exports
  every name under its HOL Light name.

  The type `:(A)group` is made with `new_type_definition`.  GROUP_RULE and
  GROUP_TAC are OCaml code: their local functions are top-level
  `partial def`s over a context of the theorems they use (as RING_RULE in
  RingTheory.lean).  `wPart` is the `W(MP_TAC o PART_MATCH ...)` idiom.

  EVALUATION ORDER.  OCaml evaluates function arguments, tuples and list
  literals right to left, so in a proof the rules nearer the end are
  evaluated first (`let ... in` lemmas first, in text order).  Where one
  proof applies two or more rules that print or use counters (MESON,
  ARITH_RULE, NUMBER_RULE, SET_RULE, TAUT, GROUP_RULE) they are bound first,
  in OCaml's order.
-/

